              "author": "Mathis Carlesso",
              "time": "8 September, 9:32 am",
              "text": "Large language models (LLMs) sometimes state things that are not true.\nThe community calls these errors \\emph{hallucinations}: the model produces fluent, confident text that is not supported by evidence or contradicts known facts \\citep{ji_survey_2023, huang_survey_2025}.\nHallucination is widely regarded as the central reliability barrier for deploying LLMs in high-stakes settings such as medicine, law, and education \\citep{lin_truthfulqa_2022}.\nConsequently, a large body of work aims to detect, measure, and reduce hallucination \\citep{li_halueval_2023, wei_measuring_2024, thorne_fever_2018, chen_menli_2023}.",
              "possiblyTruncated": false
            },
